  - [1mAlways verbose[0m — no switch needed, everything is always there. Costs console noise for every user and raises the
    stakes on redaction considerably, since every debug line ships on every session.

  [1mMy recommendation: the runtime switch.[0m It's maybe fifteen lines across both sinks and it's the specific thing that
  makes production debugging possible rather than theoretical. The one consequence to accept knowingly: a
  user-controllable debug level means debug output is effectively public, so nothing sensitive can be logged at any
  level — which is the redaction rule I'd want anyway.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should log verbosity be controlled? → Runtime switch (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I lay out the design: [1mthe redaction posture.[0m This is the one with real consequences — the logger
  will sit directly around a function that receives a password.

  - [1mAllowlist[0m — the logger emits only fields explicitly marked loggable; anything unrecognized is dropped or replaced
    with a type marker. A new field added to a payload later is silently [3mnot[0m logged until someone opts it in. Fails
    closed.
  - [1mDenylist[0m — the logger scrubs known-sensitive keys ([38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153mauthorization[39m, …) and passes everything else
    through. More convenient, more useful by default. A new sensitive field added later leaks until someone remembers to
    add it to the list. Fails open.

  [1mMy recommendation: allowlist.[0m Given you just chose a user-togglable debug level, debug output is reachable by anyone
  with devtools, and the flow in question is authentication. A denylist's failure mode here is a password in a log line;
  an allowlist's failure mode is a missing field in a log line, which you notice while debugging and fix in a minute.
  Both are cheap to write; only one is cheap to get wrong.
